<mjml>
  <mj-head>
    <mj-title>Anthias Newsletter</mj-title>

    <!-- Preview text shown next to the subject line in the inbox.
         Keep it under ~90 characters or clients truncate it. -->
    <mj-preview>Your player now warns you when the hardware is in trouble.</mj-preview>

    <!--
      NOTHING BELOW THIS HEAD MAY DEPEND ON THIS HEAD
      ===============================================
      Every colour, size, weight and space is written on the element
      that uses it. There is no mj-attributes block and no mj-class in
      this file, and tests/test_email_tokens.py fails the build if
      either comes back.

      That is not a style preference. This template had both, and the
      entire design lived in them: one mj-attributes block set the text
      colours, the section backgrounds and every padding. Strip that
      block, which is what happens the moment anything takes the body
      without the head, and MJML falls back to its own defaults: black
      text on the plum canvas at 1.6:1, no cards, and 20px of default
      padding everywhere. The result still looks like an email, which
      is what makes it dangerous. It does not look like an error.

      So the head now carries only what cannot be inlined at all: the
      title, the preview text, the web font, and one media query. Delete
      the entire mj-head and the email still renders in the right
      colours at the right sizes.

      That media query is the one cosmetic thing allowed to live up
      here, and it is worth being precise about what losing it costs.
      Stacked columns have no vertical gap between them, so the second
      card in the two-up sits directly under the first. That is the
      whole blast radius: no colour, no size and no alignment depends on
      it. Anything with a bigger blast radius belongs on the element.

      Link colours are written on each anchor rather than in a
      stylesheet for the same reason, and because several clients drop a
      <style> block outright.

      Repetition is the point. If you find yourself factoring a value
      out into one place, that place is the table below, which is
      documentation and a test fixture, not a stylesheet.

      RESOLVED TOKENS
      ---------------
      Email clients strip CSS custom properties and Outlook drops
      rgba(), so no token can be used here by name. Every one arrives
      as a literal, which makes this file a second copy of the design
      system, and a second copy goes stale in silence. It already did
      once, when the authority moved out of
      src/anthias_server/app/static/sass/_variables.scss into the
      @theme block in src/anthias_server/app/static/src/tailwind.css.

      So this table is the only place a literal may be WRITTEN DOWN as
      a decision, every use site copies from it, and the test
      re-derives every row from the real tokens, rejects any literal
      that no row accounts for, and fails on a row nothing uses.

      Rows read: TOKEN [on BACKDROP] = literal. A backdrop is named
      when the token is declared with alpha; the literal is that colour
      composited over it, since a translucent value has no meaning
      without one. Lengths are the rem token times the 16px root.

      Line heights are not listed. Each belongs to a step of the type
      ramp, so rather than a row per value the test pairs every
      font-size in this file with its line-height and checks the two
      are the same step.

      Light theme only. The app themes off an attribute written before
      first paint, which email has no equivalent of. Canvas and chrome
      are brand furniture and do not invert anyway.

        --color-canvas                              = #270035
        --color-on-canvas                           = #ffffff
        --color-on-canvas-muted  on --color-canvas  = #b3a6b8
        --color-on-canvas-faint  on --color-canvas  = #9e8ca4

        --color-surface                             = #ffffff
        --color-surface-tint                        = #f6f3f8
        --color-fg                                  = #1f002a
        --color-fg-muted                            = #5e5868
        --color-divider          on --color-surface = #ebebeb

        --color-feature-to                          = #492955
        --color-on-feature                          = #ffffff

      The footer hairline, and the one row naming a primitive rather
      than a role. There is no divider role for the canvas plane
      because the app has no hairline sitting directly on the canvas;
      sass/_styles.scss draws its footer rule on the chrome veil and
      reaches for a --color-paper-* rung there too.

        --color-paper-12         on --color-canvas  = #411f4d

        --color-accent                              = #ffe11a
        --color-accent-text                         = #1f002a

        --text-2xs                                  = 11px
        --text-xs                                   = 12px
        --text-sm                                   = 14px
        --text-base                                 = 16px
        --text-lg                                   = 18px
        --text-2xl                                  = 24px
        --text-4xl                                  = 36px
        --tracking-wider                            = 0.1em
        --radius-sm                                 = 4px
        --radius-md                                 = 8px
        --radius-lg                                 = 12px
        --font-sans   = 'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif

      Two departures from the app, neither expressible in email:

      1. The primary button is --gradient-accent in the app. Gradients
         do not render in Outlook or most webmail, so it is filled with
         --color-accent, the ramp's mid stop.
      2. The feature card is a gradient from --color-feature-from to
         --color-feature-to. Same reason; filled with the -to stop, the
         darker of the two, so --color-on-feature keeps the contrast it
         was measured at.

      And one rule about which foreground to reach for: --color-fg* is
      text on a SURFACE and flips with the theme. Text sitting directly
      on the plum canvas is always light, and takes --color-on-canvas*.
      Mixing those two up is the likeliest mistake in this file, and
      the one that produces something still readable enough to send.
    -->

    <!--
      Plus Jakarta Sans is vendored locally for the web app, but email
      cannot load /static/ fonts, so it is pulled from Google Fonts.
      Only Apple Mail and a few others honour it, which is why the full
      stack is spelled out on every element rather than relying on this.
    -->
    <mj-font
      name="Plus Jakarta Sans"
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Plus+Jakarta+Sans:wght@400;700&amp;display=swap" />

    <!-- The width at which the two-up block stops being two-up. MJML's
         default, stated so the query below and the stacking it
         compensates for cannot drift apart. -->
    <mj-breakpoint width="480px" />

    <mj-style>
      /*
        The gutter between the two cards belongs to the LEFT column's
        right edge, not split across both, because padding on the right
        column survives stacking and turns from a gutter into an indent
        under the card above it.

        Stacking loses the vertical gap instead, so the second column
        takes one back below the breakpoint only.
      */
      @media only screen and (max-width: 480px) {
        .stacked-gap { padding-top: 24px !important; }
      }
    </mj-style>
  </mj-head>

  <!-- 600px, not the 640 this started at. Two reasons, one functional
       and one typographic: 600 is the width Outlook's renderer is safe
       at and every client is built around, and at 640 the card's body
       copy ran to about 95 characters a line, well past the 45-75 a
       reader tracks comfortably. The left rail stays at 32px on every
       section so the masthead, the hero, the card and the footer all
       align down one edge. -->
  <mj-body background-color="#270035" width="600px">

    <!--
      Masthead. Gmail, Outlook and Yahoo all refuse to render SVG, so
      website/assets/images/logo-full.svg cannot be used and
      website/static/img/anthias-logo-email.png ships for this alone.
      Drawn at 2x and displayed at half, so it stays sharp on a retina
      screen, and flattened onto --color-canvas rather than left
      transparent, because older Outlook builds matte a transparent PNG
      against black. That bakes a token into an image file, so the test
      checks the PNG's own background against it.
    -->
    <mj-section background-color="#270035" padding="40px 32px 28px">
      <mj-column padding="0">
        <mj-image
          src="https://anthias.screenly.io/img/anthias-logo-email.png"
          alt="Anthias"
          width="179px"
          align="left"
          padding="0" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!--
      Hero. The short accent rule is the one piece of structure this
      email keeps, and it is an mj-divider rather than a border on a
      div because a divider compiles to a table and a border does not
      survive Word's renderer. The same device returns at full width
      inside the card, which is what makes it read as a system rather
      than as an ornament on the first screen.
    -->
    <mj-section background-color="#270035" padding="0 32px 44px">
      <mj-column padding="0">
        <mj-divider
          border-color="#ffe11a"
          border-width="2px"
          width="40px"
          align="left"
          padding="0 0 20px" />
        <mj-text
          color="#ffe11a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="12px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.4"
          letter-spacing="0.1em"
          text-transform="uppercase"
          padding="0 0 14px">
          Release {{var:release_version:"2026.08.2"}}
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#ffffff"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="36px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.15"
          padding="0 0 20px">
          Your player tells you when the hardware is in trouble
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#b3a6b8"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="18px"
          line-height="1.4"
          padding="0">
          Hello {{var:first_name:"there"}}, here is what changed in
          Anthias this month, and what it means for the screens you
          already have running.
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- Main content card -->
    <mj-section
      background-color="#ffffff"
      border-radius="12px"
      padding="40px 32px">
      <mj-column padding="0">
        <mj-text
          color="#1f002a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="24px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.3"
          padding="0 0 18px">
          Two new warnings, and both say what to do next
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#1f002a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="16px"
          line-height="1.5"
          padding="0 0 28px">
          The web interface now tells you when the power supply cannot
          hold its voltage, and when the storage card starts developing
          bad blocks. Until now the only sign of either was the screen
          itself misbehaving, which is a hard thing to trace back to a
          cable or a card. Each notice says what is wrong and what to do
          about it rather than reporting a fault, and both are readable
          on every screen and in dark mode.
        </mj-text>

        <mj-button
          align="left"
          background-color="#ffe11a"
          color="#1f002a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="16px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.5"
          border-radius="8px"
          inner-padding="14px 28px"
          padding="0"
          href="https://github.com/Screenly/Anthias/releases/tag/v2026.08.2">
          Read the release notes
        </mj-button>

        <mj-divider
          border-color="#ebebeb"
          border-width="1px"
          padding="36px 0 32px" />

        <mj-text
          color="#1f002a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="18px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.4"
          padding="0 0 8px">
          One design system behind the interface
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#5e5868"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="16px"
          line-height="1.5"
          padding="0 0 28px">
          Color, spacing and type now come from a single set of design
          tokens rather than from whichever page you happen to be on.
          Nothing moves, but everything lines up, and it holds in both
          light and dark mode.
        </mj-text>

        <mj-text
          color="#1f002a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="18px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.4"
          padding="0 0 8px">
          Smaller fixes you will notice
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#5e5868"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="16px"
          line-height="1.5"
          padding="0 0 28px">
          Field labels in the Edit asset dialog no longer sit on top of
          their example text. Turning off certificate verification now
          shows a "Not secure" badge, so it is obvious at a glance. The
          disk and memory charts use the same color for the same thing.
        </mj-text>

        <mj-text
          color="#1f002a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="18px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.4"
          padding="0 0 8px">
          How to get it
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#5e5868"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="16px"
          line-height="1.5"
          padding="0">
          Run <span style="background-color: #f6f3f8; border-radius: 4px;
          padding: 2px 6px; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular,
          Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 14px; color:
          #1f002a;">./bin/upgrade_containers.sh</span> on the device.
          Balena fleets pick the release up on their own.
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- The cards are full-bleed, so nothing separates them but this.
         An mj-spacer rather than padding on either card: padding inside
         a section with a background is filled by that background, which
         would close the gap it was added to open. -->
    <mj-section background-color="#270035" padding="0">
      <mj-column padding="0">
        <mj-spacer height="16px" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- Two-up secondary block -->
    <mj-section
      background-color="#492955"
      border-radius="12px"
      padding="32px">
      <mj-column width="50%" padding="0 24px 0 0">
        <mj-text
          color="#ffe11a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="12px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.4"
          letter-spacing="0.1em"
          text-transform="uppercase"
          padding="0 0 10px">
          From the docs
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#ffffff"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="14px"
          line-height="1.45"
          padding="0">
          <a href="https://anthias.screenly.io/docs/balena-fleet-deployment/"
             style="color: #ffe11a; text-decoration: underline;">
            Deploying a Balena fleet
          </a><br />
          Updating every screen you run without touching them one by one.
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
      <mj-column width="50%" padding="0" css-class="stacked-gap">
        <mj-text
          color="#ffe11a"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="12px"
          font-weight="700"
          line-height="1.4"
          letter-spacing="0.1em"
          text-transform="uppercase"
          padding="0 0 10px">
          Community
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#ffffff"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="14px"
          line-height="1.45"
          padding="0">
          <a href="https://forums.screenly.io/c/anthias/"
             style="color: #ffe11a; text-decoration: underline;">
            The Anthias forum
          </a><br />
          Questions, hardware reports, and what people are building.
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- Footer -->
    <mj-section background-color="#270035" padding="44px 32px 20px">
      <mj-column padding="0">
        <mj-divider
          border-color="#411f4d"
          border-width="1px"
          padding="0 0 26px" />
        <mj-text
          color="#b3a6b8"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="12px"
          line-height="1.4"
          padding="0 0 18px">
          <a href="https://anthias.screenly.io/"
             style="color: #b3a6b8; text-decoration: underline;">Website</a>
          &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
          <a href="https://anthias.screenly.io/docs/"
             style="color: #b3a6b8; text-decoration: underline;">Documentation</a>
          &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
          <a href="https://github.com/Screenly/Anthias"
             style="color: #b3a6b8; text-decoration: underline;">GitHub</a>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#9e8ca4"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="11px"
          line-height="1.45"
          padding="0 0 12px">
          Anthias is free and open source software, maintained by
          Screenly and its contributors.
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#9e8ca4"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="11px"
          line-height="1.45"
          padding="0 0 12px">
          Screenly Inc, 16192 Coastal Hwy., Lewes, DE 19958, United States
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          color="#9e8ca4"
          font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif"
          font-size="11px"
          line-height="1.45"
          padding="0">
          You are receiving this because you signed up for Anthias
          release notes.
          <a href="[[UNSUB_LINK_EN]]"
             style="color: #b3a6b8; text-decoration: underline;">Unsubscribe</a>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

  </mj-body>
</mjml>
